@page "/delete-data"
@* How to delete what Stikling keeps. Facebook's data deletion setting links here, so like the privacy page
   it answers 200 on the live site (see prepare-pages.py) and has to match the Account page. *@

<PageTitle>Deleting your data · Stikling</PageTitle>

<a class="back-link" href="privacy">Privacy</a>
<h1>Deleting your data</h1>

<section class="delete-data-section">
    <h2>With an account</h2>
    <p>
        It works the same whether you signed in with Google, Facebook, Discord or your email.
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>Open Stikling at <a href="https://stikling.app">stikling.app</a> and tap the gear at the top.</li>
        <li>Tap your account at the top of Settings, the row with your email.</li>
        <li>Tap <em>Delete account</em> at the bottom, and confirm.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        Your plants, photos and settings are erased from the Stikling server straight away, and Clerk deletes
        your sign-in. The database's automatic backups still hold a copy for up to 7 days, after which it's
        gone. You choose whether the device you do it on keeps what it has.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you can't sign in any more, write to
        <a href="mailto:support@stikling.app">support@stikling.app</a> from the email address on the account
        and ask for it to be deleted.
    </p>
    <p>
        Stikling also shows up in the list of apps your Facebook, Google or Discord account has signed in to.
        You can remove it from that list in the account's own settings.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="delete-data-section">
    <h2>Without an account</h2>
    <p>
        Everything is on your device and nothing is on the server. Clearing the site data for stikling.app in
        your browser, or deleting the installed app, removes all of it.
    </p>
</section>

<p class="delete-data-more">
    The <a href="privacy">privacy page</a> says what is kept and where.
</p>
